{"authored" [{"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814c19e51d37a9c9f69a55428cd1cb7bf2793818f57cd8d02c50fdbd1af6807e" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "c879fecd06cffc5b43ab2030f581fd917f356896b4b27d5e796c6255203792a1" "basis" 1417 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f13d2282a7ffd90ddf962ee3303863fde4fb53d68c44ca745d6277337811ca24"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-4"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=4"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=6{x}^{3}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18{x}^{2}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 36(\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{{x}^{4}}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =36(-2)+\\frac{36}{(-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{279}{4}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)+\\frac{36}{(1)^{4}}=72>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f13d2282a7ffd90ddf962ee3303863fde4fb53d68c44ca745d6277337811ca24"} {"question" "q-21044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-1"] ["math" "x=-3"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=1"] ["math" "x=6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21044" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-3x+7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.  \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=2$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2(0)-2=-2<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =2(2)-2=2>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f13d2282a7ffd90ddf962ee3303863fde4fb53d68c44ca745d6277337811ca24"} {"question" "q-21045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=1"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=3"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-21045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-79203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/2c0a892cd217758e2871f2d3f6f18d7615e79e0f5ad792c751c662e35c69e168.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-79203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-79203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f13d2282a7ffd90ddf962ee3303863fde4fb53d68c44ca745d6277337811ca24"} {"question" "q-79203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-79203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=2"] ["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=5$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-79203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-114419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/036e249b188b46fe94bd6955fce114cb11dc0f1e1c169fb58d36135cfbc9b797.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-114419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has no inflection points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-114419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f13d2282a7ffd90ddf962ee3303863fde4fb53d68c44ca745d6277337811ca24"} {"question" "q-114419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-114419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$g(x)$ has no inflection points"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/state.json" "file_sha256" "3efd6f389e00f28bd8f84e26d37396500d4cce43d652b664bbccef044481c89e"} {"question" "q-114419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$g(x)$ has no inflection points"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/st